<!DOCTYPE article PUBLIC "-//NLM//DTD Journal Archiving and Interchange DTD v2.3 20070202//EN" "archivearticle.dtd"><article xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" article-type="research-article"><?properties open_access?><front><journal-meta><journal-id journal-id-type="nlm-ta">J Exp Med</journal-id><journal-title>The Journal of Experimental Medicine</journal-title><issn pub-type="ppub">0022-1007</issn><issn pub-type="epub">1540-9538</issn><publisher><publisher-name>The Rockefeller University Press</publisher-name></publisher></journal-meta><article-meta><article-id pub-id-type="pmid">19870101</article-id><article-id pub-id-type="pmc">2132206</article-id><article-categories><subj-group subj-group-type="heading"><subject>Article</subject></subj-group></article-categories><title-group><article-title>STUDIES ON BARTONELLA MURIS ANEMIA </article-title><subtitle>VI. A LIPOID EXTRACT OF THE SPLEEN THAT PREVENTS BARTONELLA MURIS ANEMIA IN SPLENECTOMIZED ALBINO RATS</subtitle></title-group><contrib-group><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Perla</surname><given-names>David</given-names></name></contrib><contrib contrib-type="author"><name><surname>Marmorston-Gottesman</surname><given-names>J.</given-names></name></contrib></contrib-group><aff id="N0x327dab0N0x39209f0">From the Laboratory Division, Montefiore Hospital, New York</aff><pub-date pub-type="ppub"><day>30</day><month>11</month><year>1932</year></pub-date><volume>56</volume><issue>6</issue><fpage>777</fpage><lpage>782</lpage><history><date date-type="received"><day>8</day><month>7</month><year>1932</year></date></history><permissions><copyright-statement>Copyright &#x000a9; Copyright, 1932, by The Rockefeller Institute for Medical Research New York</copyright-statement></permissions><abstract><p> An aqueous lipoid extract of ox spleen was prepared which protects adult male albino rats of carrier stock in a large percentage of instances against <italic>Bartonella muris</italic> anemia following splenectomy. It is suggested that the extract contains a specific hormonal substance. </p></abstract></article-meta></front></article>